12
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,896
And her thumb is hovering over the

13
00:00:18,896 --> 00:00:20,104
buy button.

14
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:22,930
Day one was a roller coaster.

15
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:25,651
The stock opened far above the offer,

16
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:27,228
slid back all afternoon,

17
00:00:27,228 --> 00:00:30,800
and closed at about ninety-seven dollars.

18
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:33,040
Everyone on her feed has an opinion.

19
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,240
Maya has a rule instead.

20
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:37,484
Never buy something before you've read the

21
00:00:37,484 --> 00:00:38,492
label.

22
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:40,721
So she grabs a clipboard and writes

23
00:00:40,721 --> 00:00:43,510
down the questions a careful buyer asks.

24
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,410
What do I actually own?

25
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:46,590
What am I paying?

26
00:00:46,590 --> 00:00:48,190
When can insiders sell?

27
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:49,990
What will the first report show?

28
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:51,850
Who does this company depend on?

29
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:53,860
How wild do new stocks get?

30
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:55,470
And who's really in charge?

31
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:57,033
Let's go through them with her.

32
00:00:57,483 --> 00:01:04,233
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

33
00:01:10,083 --> 00:01:11,953
A quick note first.

34
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:14,643
This is not investment advice.

35
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:15,579
We won't tell you to buy or

36
00:01:15,579 --> 00:01:15,817
sell,

37
00:01:15,817 --> 00:01:17,153
and we have no price target.

38
00:01:17,153 --> 00:01:20,083
Everything here comes from Anthropic's

39
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:21,122
prospectus,

40
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:23,786
other public filings and named news

41
00:01:23,786 --> 00:01:24,558
reports,

42
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:27,023
with our own math labelled.

43
00:01:27,203 --> 00:01:29,078
Our channel uses A I tools,

44
00:01:29,078 --> 00:01:30,110
including Claude,

45
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:31,563
to make its videos.

46
00:01:31,563 --> 00:01:33,686
Anthropic didn't sponsor or review this

47
00:01:33,686 --> 00:01:34,089
one,

48
00:01:34,089 --> 00:01:36,000
which is exactly why we stick to

49
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,237
the filings.

50
00:01:42,883 --> 00:01:44,623
First on the list.

51
00:01:44,623 --> 00:01:46,313
What do you actually own?

52
00:01:46,313 --> 00:01:48,653
Anthropic doesn't have just one kind of

53
00:01:48,653 --> 00:01:49,493
stock.

54
00:01:49,493 --> 00:01:51,263
It has a whole family.

55
00:01:51,263 --> 00:01:53,351
Class A is what trades on the

56
00:01:53,351 --> 00:01:54,108
Nasdaq,

57
00:01:54,108 --> 00:01:55,963
with one vote per share.

58
00:01:55,963 --> 00:01:57,785
Class N gets the same money,

59
00:01:57,785 --> 00:01:58,685
but no vote,

60
00:01:58,685 --> 00:02:00,992
and it's held mostly by big strategic

61
00:02:00,992 --> 00:02:02,013
partners.

62
00:02:02,013 --> 00:02:03,701
And then come the special classes that

63
00:02:03,701 --> 00:02:04,619
carry power,

64
00:02:04,619 --> 00:02:06,345
but no money at all.

65
00:02:06,583 --> 00:02:08,103
Here's the twist.

66
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:09,416
A single Class F share,

67
00:02:09,416 --> 00:02:11,508
held by a company the co-founders own,

68
00:02:11,508 --> 00:02:13,265
carries just over half of the votes

69
00:02:13,265 --> 00:02:15,623
at every shareholder meeting.

70
00:02:16,193 --> 00:02:17,696
A single Class T share,

71
00:02:17,696 --> 00:02:19,634
held by an independent trust,

72
00:02:19,634 --> 00:02:21,323
picks most of the board.

73
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:23,092
So a Class A buyer does get

74
00:02:23,092 --> 00:02:24,093
a vote.

75
00:02:24,093 --> 00:02:26,336
But the big decisions are already spoken

76
00:02:26,336 --> 00:02:27,123
for.

77
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:27,807
Next,

78
00:02:27,807 --> 00:02:30,413
how many shares really exist.

79
00:02:30,553 --> 00:02:32,311
The offering put a big block of

80
00:02:32,311 --> 00:02:34,083
stock into public hands.

81
00:02:34,173 --> 00:02:36,071
But that block is fewer than one

82
00:02:36,071 --> 00:02:39,043
in ten of all the shares outstanding.

83
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:41,378
Most of the company still belongs to

84
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:42,691
early investors,

85
00:02:42,691 --> 00:02:44,403
partners and employees.

86
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:45,661
And for now,

87
00:02:45,661 --> 00:02:47,580
most of them can't sell.

88
00:02:47,817 --> 00:02:49,607
Let me draw the difference between

89
00:02:49,607 --> 00:02:51,997
outstanding and fully diluted.

90
00:02:52,017 --> 00:02:54,040
Outstanding shares are the ones that exist

91
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:54,977
today.

92
00:02:55,077 --> 00:02:57,108
Now add the employee stock options,

93
00:02:57,108 --> 00:02:58,911
the stock awards still vesting,

94
00:02:58,911 --> 00:03:01,192
and the notes Amazon holds that turn

95
00:03:01,192 --> 00:03:02,477
into stock.

96
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:04,323
That's the fully diluted count,

97
00:03:04,323 --> 00:03:06,807
about a third more shares than today.

98
00:03:07,047 --> 00:03:09,509
So when someone quotes a market value,

99
00:03:09,509 --> 00:03:11,464
ask which count they used.

100
00:03:11,717 --> 00:03:14,337
And that count can keep growing.

101
00:03:14,447 --> 00:03:16,528
Amazon's notes convert a slice at a

102
00:03:16,528 --> 00:03:16,887
time,

103
00:03:16,887 --> 00:03:18,003
every few months,

104
00:03:18,003 --> 00:03:19,927
up to an ownership cap.

105
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:23,287
Employee plans add new stock every year.

106
00:03:23,437 --> 00:03:25,025
And there's a twist you won't find

107
00:03:25,025 --> 00:03:26,617
at most companies.

108
00:03:26,867 --> 00:03:27,791
Each time the share price hits a

109
00:03:27,791 --> 00:03:28,438
new milestone,

110
00:03:28,438 --> 00:03:29,627
the board may hand extra shares to

111
00:03:29,627 --> 00:03:31,077
Anthropic's own public benefit fund.

112
00:03:31,077 --> 00:03:34,757
Good for its mission.

113
00:03:34,757 --> 00:03:35,714
A slightly smaller slice for everyone

114
00:03:35,714 --> 00:03:36,410
else.

115
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:38,500
So that's what you own.

116
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:39,045
Now,

117
00:03:39,045 --> 00:03:41,368
what are you paying for it?

118
00:03:47,017 --> 00:03:49,227
Next on the list.

119
00:03:49,367 --> 00:03:50,447
The price.

120
00:03:50,447 --> 00:03:53,405
Anthropic barely makes an operating profit

121
00:03:53,405 --> 00:03:53,780
yet,

122
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:55,753
so the usual price to earnings ratio

123
00:03:55,753 --> 00:03:56,747
doesn't work.

124
00:03:56,857 --> 00:03:59,867
Investors use price to sales instead.

125
00:04:00,117 --> 00:04:01,352
Take the latest close,

126
00:04:01,352 --> 00:04:03,634
multiply by the shares outstanding,

127
00:04:03,634 --> 00:04:05,308
and divide by the last year of

128
00:04:05,308 --> 00:04:06,327
revenue.

129
00:04:06,487 --> 00:04:07,323
By our math,

130
00:04:07,323 --> 00:04:10,065
that's about thirty-six times sales.

131
00:04:10,317 --> 00:04:11,691
Let me put that next to the

132
00:04:11,691 --> 00:04:12,277
giants.

133
00:04:12,277 --> 00:04:13,447
Here's Nvidia.

134
00:04:13,447 --> 00:04:14,847
Here's Microsoft.

135
00:04:14,847 --> 00:04:16,146
On the same day's close,

136
00:04:16,146 --> 00:04:17,225
with the same math,

137
00:04:17,225 --> 00:04:19,207
both sit at about ten times their

138
00:04:19,207 --> 00:04:20,107
sales.

139
00:04:20,107 --> 00:04:21,054
And here's A,

140
00:04:21,054 --> 00:04:21,421
N,

141
00:04:21,421 --> 00:04:21,788
T,

142
00:04:21,788 --> 00:04:23,397
towering over them.

143
00:04:23,397 --> 00:04:24,747
Same yardstick.

144
00:04:24,747 --> 00:04:26,545
Very different height.

145
00:04:26,783 --> 00:04:28,333
Is that crazy?

146
00:04:28,393 --> 00:04:30,133
Not automatically.

147
00:04:30,233 --> 00:04:31,961
The giants grow far more slowly,

148
00:04:31,961 --> 00:04:34,148
and Anthropic's revenue so far this year

149
00:04:34,148 --> 00:04:36,117
is many times what it was a

150
00:04:36,117 --> 00:04:37,373
year earlier.

151
00:04:37,713 --> 00:04:39,872
Measured against its latest monthly pace,

152
00:04:39,872 --> 00:04:41,123
stretched over a year,

153
00:04:41,123 --> 00:04:42,993
the multiple shrinks a lot.

154
00:04:43,113 --> 00:04:44,793
That's the bull case.

155
00:04:45,003 --> 00:04:46,743
The bear case is simple.

156
00:04:46,873 --> 00:04:48,872
A price that high only works if

157
00:04:48,872 --> 00:04:50,346
fast growth lasts for years,

158
00:04:50,346 --> 00:04:53,445
and fast growth rarely lasts forever.

159
00:04:53,683 --> 00:04:55,683
One more label to check.

160
00:04:55,683 --> 00:04:57,862
Anthropic holds a big pile of cash

161
00:04:57,862 --> 00:04:59,148
from its private rounds,

162
00:04:59,148 --> 00:05:01,305
plus the money from this offering,

163
00:05:01,305 --> 00:05:03,213
and very little debt.

164
00:05:03,303 --> 00:05:04,722
So its enterprise value,

165
00:05:04,722 --> 00:05:06,758
the price of the business itself,

166
00:05:06,758 --> 00:05:09,003
is lower than its market value.

167
00:05:09,033 --> 00:05:11,563
Our multiple uses market value.

168
00:05:11,563 --> 00:05:13,302
Whatever headline you read,

169
00:05:13,302 --> 00:05:15,222
check which one it uses.

170
00:05:20,883 --> 00:05:22,133
Next question.

171
00:05:22,133 --> 00:05:24,033
When can insiders sell?

172
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:26,653
Most new stocks come with a lock-up.

173
00:05:26,653 --> 00:05:28,645
Early holders promise not to sell for

174
00:05:28,645 --> 00:05:29,803
a while.

175
00:05:29,803 --> 00:05:32,073
Anthropic's isn't one cliff.

176
00:05:32,073 --> 00:05:33,433
It's a staircase.

177
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:35,548
Some employee shares could trade from day

178
00:05:35,548 --> 00:05:36,293
one.

179
00:05:36,293 --> 00:05:38,525
More unlock in steps through December,

180
00:05:38,525 --> 00:05:40,563
January and February.

181
00:05:40,563 --> 00:05:42,577
And a bigger wave follows right after

182
00:05:42,577 --> 00:05:44,385
the first annual results.

183
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:46,199
Then comes the big gate,

184
00:05:46,199 --> 00:05:48,510
the one most investors will circle.

185
00:05:48,510 --> 00:05:50,684
It opens right after the results for

186
00:05:50,684 --> 00:05:51,966
January to March,

187
00:05:51,966 --> 00:05:53,922
or at the very latest on May

188
00:05:53,922 --> 00:05:55,609
seventeenth next year.

189
00:05:55,850 --> 00:05:57,689
Behind that gate sit up to three

190
00:05:57,689 --> 00:05:58,816
billion shares,

191
00:05:58,816 --> 00:06:01,115
the biggest single step on the whole

192
00:06:01,115 --> 00:06:02,405
calendar.

193
00:06:02,650 --> 00:06:03,727
The co-founders,

194
00:06:03,727 --> 00:06:06,144
and investors holding more than a sliver

195
00:06:06,144 --> 00:06:07,441
of the company,

196
00:06:07,441 --> 00:06:08,600
wait longer.

197
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:09,918
Up to a full year,

198
00:06:09,918 --> 00:06:12,152
until the results for next summer are

199
00:06:12,152 --> 00:06:12,576
out,

200
00:06:12,576 --> 00:06:15,857
or the following November at the latest.

201
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:18,627
Unlocked doesn't mean sold.

202
00:06:18,787 --> 00:06:20,777
Plenty of holders keep their shares.

203
00:06:20,777 --> 00:06:22,029
But more shares that can be sold

204
00:06:22,029 --> 00:06:23,265
means more possible sellers,

205
00:06:23,265 --> 00:06:25,007
so Maya writes these dates down.

206
00:06:25,007 --> 00:06:26,817
One more detail.

207
00:06:26,817 --> 00:06:29,057
The chip and cloud companies that bought

208
00:06:29,057 --> 00:06:32,293
shares privately at the offer price aren't

209
00:06:32,293 --> 00:06:34,387
in the lock-up at all.

210
00:06:34,487 --> 00:06:36,122
But their shares are restricted,

211
00:06:36,122 --> 00:06:37,591
so they can't simply sell them on

212
00:06:37,591 --> 00:06:39,660
the market whenever they like.

213
00:06:45,317 --> 00:06:45,917
Next,

214
00:06:45,917 --> 00:06:48,097
the first report card.

215
00:06:48,377 --> 00:06:50,502
Anthropic's first results as a public

216
00:06:50,502 --> 00:06:52,605
company will cover this full year,

217
00:06:52,605 --> 00:06:55,117
and they should arrive early next year.

218
00:06:55,397 --> 00:06:57,517
The date hasn't been announced yet.

219
00:06:57,577 --> 00:06:59,247
But it matters twice.

220
00:06:59,467 --> 00:07:00,762
It's the first fresh look at the

221
00:07:00,762 --> 00:07:02,567
numbers since the offering.

222
00:07:02,567 --> 00:07:04,147
And it's also the trigger for one

223
00:07:04,147 --> 00:07:05,801
of those lock-up waves.

224
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:08,630
Here's what Maya will look for.

225
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:09,137
First,

226
00:07:09,137 --> 00:07:09,870
revenue growth.

227
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:11,180
Is the rocket still climbing?

228
00:07:11,180 --> 00:07:11,756
Second,

229
00:07:11,756 --> 00:07:13,150
the compute bill.

230
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:16,380
How much do chips and data centers

231
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:19,700
cost for each dollar of sales?

232
00:07:19,790 --> 00:07:19,869
Third,

233
00:07:19,869 --> 00:07:20,540
operating cash flow.

234
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:24,140
Is real cash still coming in?

235
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:24,682
And fourth,

236
00:07:24,682 --> 00:07:26,450
the commitment schedule.

237
00:07:26,450 --> 00:07:28,462
How much has it promised to spend,

238
00:07:28,462 --> 00:07:29,469
and when?

239
00:07:29,717 --> 00:07:32,019
That compute bill is the one to

240
00:07:32,019 --> 00:07:32,677
watch.

241
00:07:32,677 --> 00:07:33,175
Last year,

242
00:07:33,175 --> 00:07:34,722
it was bigger than the sales it

243
00:07:34,722 --> 00:07:35,417
helped earn.

244
00:07:35,417 --> 00:07:36,434
So far this year,

245
00:07:36,434 --> 00:07:38,717
it's down to about fifty-seven cents for

246
00:07:38,717 --> 00:07:40,977
every dollar of revenue.

247
00:07:41,017 --> 00:07:42,267
If that keeps falling,

248
00:07:42,267 --> 00:07:43,927
margins open up.

249
00:07:43,927 --> 00:07:44,970
If it climbs back,

250
00:07:44,970 --> 00:07:46,847
the story gets harder.

251
00:07:47,083 --> 00:07:49,433
And then there are the promises.

252
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:51,556
Anthropic has signed up to spend about

253
00:07:51,556 --> 00:07:53,678
nine hundred billion dollars on computing

254
00:07:53,678 --> 00:07:55,782
and data centers over many years,

255
00:07:55,782 --> 00:07:57,904
much of it owed whether customers show

256
00:07:57,904 --> 00:07:59,083
up or not.

257
00:07:59,233 --> 00:08:00,429
On the other side,

258
00:08:00,429 --> 00:08:02,031
it has a large cash cushion,

259
00:08:02,031 --> 00:08:04,059
and its operating cash flow turned

260
00:08:04,059 --> 00:08:05,673
positive this year.

261
00:08:05,793 --> 00:08:07,964
The report will show whether that cash

262
00:08:07,964 --> 00:08:09,770
keeps pace with the bills.

263
00:08:15,417 --> 00:08:16,497
Next.

264
00:08:16,497 --> 00:08:18,657
Who does Anthropic depend on?

265
00:08:18,657 --> 00:08:20,977
Start with Amazon and Google.

266
00:08:20,977 --> 00:08:23,117
Both are big shareholders.

267
00:08:23,117 --> 00:08:25,175
Both rent it the computing power it

268
00:08:25,175 --> 00:08:26,187
runs on.

269
00:08:26,317 --> 00:08:28,053
Both sell Claude to their own cloud

270
00:08:28,053 --> 00:08:29,397
customers.

271
00:08:29,437 --> 00:08:31,181
And both build A I models of

272
00:08:31,181 --> 00:08:32,217
their own.

273
00:08:32,247 --> 00:08:32,917
Partner,

274
00:08:32,917 --> 00:08:33,587
landlord,

275
00:08:33,587 --> 00:08:35,182
salesman and rival,

276
00:08:35,182 --> 00:08:36,475
all at once.

277
00:08:36,717 --> 00:08:38,547
Let me draw how that works.

278
00:08:38,547 --> 00:08:40,627
Amazon invests in Anthropic.

279
00:08:40,627 --> 00:08:43,337
Anthropic pays Amazon for computing.

280
00:08:43,337 --> 00:08:45,627
Amazon's cloud customers buy Claude,

281
00:08:45,627 --> 00:08:47,847
and Amazon keeps a cut.

282
00:08:48,097 --> 00:08:50,557
Google sits in a similar triangle.

283
00:08:50,577 --> 00:08:52,532
The prospectus itself warns that these

284
00:08:52,532 --> 00:08:54,487
ties could create conflicts of interest

285
00:08:54,487 --> 00:08:57,259
and affect its access to computing.

286
00:08:57,517 --> 00:08:59,807
The reassuring part is in the chips.

287
00:08:59,807 --> 00:09:01,869
Anthropic trains and runs Claude on chips

288
00:09:01,869 --> 00:09:02,991
from Amazon,

289
00:09:02,991 --> 00:09:03,656
Google,

290
00:09:03,656 --> 00:09:05,236
Nvidia and A M D,

291
00:09:05,236 --> 00:09:07,298
so no single supplier can choke it

292
00:09:07,298 --> 00:09:08,027
off.

293
00:09:08,027 --> 00:09:09,007
The flip side?

294
00:09:09,007 --> 00:09:10,983
Several of those suppliers are also

295
00:09:10,983 --> 00:09:11,952
shareholders,

296
00:09:11,952 --> 00:09:14,331
and its commitments to them are huge.

297
00:09:14,583 --> 00:09:16,213
And the customers?

298
00:09:16,213 --> 00:09:17,420
So far this year,

299
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:20,129
no single customer was big enough to

300
00:09:20,129 --> 00:09:22,103
be named in the filing.

301
00:09:22,103 --> 00:09:22,389
Last year,

302
00:09:22,389 --> 00:09:23,383
a couple were.

303
00:09:23,733 --> 00:09:25,583
That's a healthier spread.

304
00:09:25,803 --> 00:09:27,552
But a big share of sales still

305
00:09:27,552 --> 00:09:29,519
flows through the cloud marketplaces of

306
00:09:29,519 --> 00:09:30,863
those same partners.

307
00:09:30,863 --> 00:09:32,766
So the web of ties runs both

308
00:09:32,766 --> 00:09:33,638
ways.

309
00:09:39,283 --> 00:09:40,533
Next.

310
00:09:40,543 --> 00:09:42,973
How wild can a new stock get?

311
00:09:43,113 --> 00:09:45,283
Look back at Facebook's debut.

312
00:09:45,283 --> 00:09:46,438
Within a few months,

313
00:09:46,438 --> 00:09:48,396
its shares had fallen to about half

314
00:09:48,396 --> 00:09:49,356
the offer price,

315
00:09:49,356 --> 00:09:51,118
and the first lock-up ended right in

316
00:09:51,118 --> 00:09:52,893
the middle of that slide.

317
00:09:52,913 --> 00:09:54,555
A year after the offering,

318
00:09:54,555 --> 00:09:56,631
it was still below the offer.

319
00:09:56,883 --> 00:09:58,933
Now the opposite story.

320
00:09:58,963 --> 00:10:01,296
Chip designer Arm listed in the fall,

321
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:02,559
popped on day one,

322
00:10:02,559 --> 00:10:05,443
then slipped below its offer within weeks.

323
00:10:05,443 --> 00:10:06,314
Months later,

324
00:10:06,314 --> 00:10:08,524
a strong quarterly report came out,

325
00:10:08,524 --> 00:10:10,533
and the stock jumped by nearly half

326
00:10:10,533 --> 00:10:12,249
in a single day.

327
00:10:12,483 --> 00:10:14,483
Same lesson from both.

328
00:10:14,483 --> 00:10:15,507
In its first year,

329
00:10:15,507 --> 00:10:17,596
a new stock trades on very little

330
00:10:17,596 --> 00:10:18,793
history.

331
00:10:18,793 --> 00:10:20,373
In both of these cases,

332
00:10:20,373 --> 00:10:22,662
some of the biggest moves came around

333
00:10:22,662 --> 00:10:25,203
lock-up dates and earnings reports.

334
00:10:25,203 --> 00:10:27,341
Those are exactly the dates on Maya's

335
00:10:27,341 --> 00:10:28,369
clipboard.

336
00:10:34,017 --> 00:10:35,207
Last question.

337
00:10:35,207 --> 00:10:36,817
Who governs this company?

338
00:10:36,817 --> 00:10:39,857
Anthropic is a public benefit corporation.

339
00:10:39,857 --> 00:10:40,412
By law,

340
00:10:40,412 --> 00:10:42,519
its board must balance shareholders' money

341
00:10:42,519 --> 00:10:44,818
with the interests of people affected by

342
00:10:44,818 --> 00:10:45,373
its work,

343
00:10:45,373 --> 00:10:46,887
and with a stated mission,

344
00:10:46,887 --> 00:10:49,568
the responsible development of advanced A

345
00:10:49,568 --> 00:10:52,658
I for the long-term benefit of humanity.

346
00:10:52,917 --> 00:10:55,071
On top of that sits the Long-Term

347
00:10:55,071 --> 00:10:56,390
Benefit Trust,

348
00:10:56,390 --> 00:10:58,544
a small group of trustees with no

349
00:10:58,544 --> 00:11:01,567
financial stake in the company.

350
00:11:01,967 --> 00:11:02,498
Through that Class T share,

351
00:11:02,498 --> 00:11:03,507
they elect most of the board.

352
00:11:03,507 --> 00:11:06,301
That makes Anthropic what Nasdaq calls a

353
00:11:06,301 --> 00:11:08,054
controlled company,

354
00:11:08,054 --> 00:11:10,087
which may skip some of the usual

355
00:11:10,087 --> 00:11:11,957
governance rules.

356
00:11:12,217 --> 00:11:14,177
What does that mean for a buyer?

357
00:11:14,207 --> 00:11:15,994
Supporters say it shields long-term

358
00:11:15,994 --> 00:11:18,157
thinking from short-term pressure.

359
00:11:18,197 --> 00:11:19,988
The prospectus itself warns that the trust

360
00:11:19,988 --> 00:11:21,939
may have interests that differ from yours,

361
00:11:21,939 --> 00:11:23,372
and could use its power in ways

362
00:11:23,372 --> 00:11:24,867
you disagree with.

363
00:11:25,007 --> 00:11:25,704
Either way,

364
00:11:25,704 --> 00:11:26,401
if you buy,

365
00:11:26,401 --> 00:11:28,188
you're joining a company where your vote

366
00:11:28,188 --> 00:11:29,324
has limits.

367
00:11:34,983 --> 00:11:35,442
So,

368
00:11:35,442 --> 00:11:37,493
what would change the picture?

369
00:11:37,583 --> 00:11:38,608
On the bright side,

370
00:11:38,608 --> 00:11:40,680
watch for revenue that keeps growing fast,

371
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:42,563
a compute bill that keeps shrinking for

372
00:11:42,563 --> 00:11:44,425
every dollar of sales,

373
00:11:44,425 --> 00:11:46,078
cash flow that stays positive,

374
00:11:46,078 --> 00:11:48,171
and lock-up waves that pass without a

375
00:11:48,171 --> 00:11:49,608
flood of selling.

376
00:11:49,850 --> 00:11:50,964
On the dark side,

377
00:11:50,964 --> 00:11:52,760
watch for growth that slows sharply,

378
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:54,784
a compute bill that climbs back up,

379
00:11:54,784 --> 00:11:56,375
cash that can't keep pace with the

380
00:11:56,375 --> 00:11:57,262
commitments,

381
00:11:57,262 --> 00:11:59,536
or big holders heading for the exit

382
00:11:59,536 --> 00:12:01,500
when the gates open.

383
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:04,900
Maya pins the clipboard to her fridge.

384
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:06,460
She didn't decide today,

385
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:08,810
and we're not deciding for her.

386
00:12:09,070 --> 00:12:10,848
But now she knows what she would

387
00:12:10,848 --> 00:12:11,331
own,

388
00:12:11,331 --> 00:12:12,576
what she would pay,

389
00:12:12,576 --> 00:12:14,700
and which dates to watch.

390
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:16,704
The prices in this video are from

391
00:12:16,704 --> 00:12:17,675
the first day's close,

392
00:12:17,675 --> 00:12:19,855
and every figure is sourced in the

393
00:12:19,855 --> 00:12:21,050
description.

394
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:22,262
And once more,

395
00:12:22,262 --> 00:12:24,919
this is not investment advice.

396
00:12:25,183 --> 00:12:26,640
Next in the series,

